# MemoryMin: the app user's session host survives memory pressure.
#
# What MemoryMin buys. Under memory pressure the kernel reclaims pages from
# the least-protected cgroup first. The t3 server, its cloudflared connector
# and every agent session live in this unit's cgroup — exactly the processes
# whose stalls read as "connection dropped" from the outside. MemoryMin is
# what makes earlyoom's job easier without either knowing about the other:
# reclaim hits the build daemons and browser tabs first, and this cgroup's
# first 400 MB are exempt.
#
# It is a shield, not a killer: MemoryMin never *causes* pressure and never
# kills anything. earlyoom (system-level, the earlyoom role) is the response
# when memory is genuinely gone; this drop-in is what keeps the tunnel and
# session host healthy while earlyoom works.
#
# Why a drop-in and not our own unit: docs/design.md, "The server runs under
# t3's own unit" — the repo never writes t3code.service itself, because t3
# owns that file and tracks its own changes. drop-in/ needs none of t3's
# install machinery and survives a 't3 service update' that rewrites the unit.
# systemd's own extension point: the directory and file below it are ours.
#
# KillMode, OOMPolicy and Restart stay t3's; OOMPolicy=continue is already
# right: a greedy child killed by the kernel or earlyoom must not take the
# whole session host down with it.

[Service]
MemoryMin={{ t3_service_memory_min }}
